{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-4636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x+6}{x-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-4636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x+6}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{x}{x}+\\frac{6}{x})}{(\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+1+\\frac{6}{x})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{6}{x}$ and $\\frac{1}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(2x+1+\\frac{6}{x})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{2x+1+0}{1-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{1} \\\\  & =2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-4636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0bda81e946e244a0e6c7bcb8320fb2b7a0d487c4e7828171b2bed5bac39f0411"} {"question" "q-4636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-4636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-2"] ["math" "y=0"] ["math" "y=1"] ["math" "y=2"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-4636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No horizontal asymptotes"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{3{x}^{4}-x+3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{3{x}^{4}-x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}$ and $\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-0}{3-0+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0bda81e946e244a0e6c7bcb8320fb2b7a0d487c4e7828171b2bed5bac39f0411"} {"question" "q-113169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-1"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "y=0"] ["math" "y=3"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{7x+3}{6{x}^{7}-2{x}^{2}-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{7}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{7},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{7x+3}{6{x}^{7}-2{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7x}{{x}^{7}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{7}}{{x}^{7}}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{7}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{{x}^{6}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}})}{(6-\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{7}{{x}^{6}},\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}},\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{{x}^{6}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}})}{(6-\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{6-0-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0bda81e946e244a0e6c7bcb8320fb2b7a0d487c4e7828171b2bed5bac39f0411"} {"question" "q-113173" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113173" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-1"] ["math" "y=-3"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "y=0"] ["math" "y=\\frac{7}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113173" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113176" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{8}-4{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{6}-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113176" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{6}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{8}-4{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{6}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{{x}^{6}}-\\frac{4{x}^{4}}{{x}^{6}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{6}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},$ $\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{{x}^{2}-0+0}{1-0} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113176" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0bda81e946e244a0e6c7bcb8320fb2b7a0d487c4e7828171b2bed5bac39f0411"} {"question" "q-113176" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113176" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-1"] ["math" "y=-2"] ["math" "y=0"] ["math" "y=1"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113176" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No horizontal asymptotes"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{8{x}^{6}+3{x}^{2}-7}{{x}^{4}-6x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{8{x}^{6}+3{x}^{2}-7}{{x}^{4}-6x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8{x}^{6}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{6x}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(8{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})}{(1-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},$ $\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}}$ and $\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(8{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})}{(1-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{8{x}^{2}+0-0}{1-0} \\\\  & =8{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0bda81e946e244a0e6c7bcb8320fb2b7a0d487c4e7828171b2bed5bac39f0411"} {"question" "q-113183" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113183" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-1"] ["math" "y=-3"] ["math" "y=8"] ["math" "y=\\frac{7}{6}"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json" "file_sha256" "b034e39183d3666cb0c76bff629b7268686af984b316ecaa02379a0d6b2802d2"} {"question" "q-113183" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No horizontal asymptotes"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068696/state.json"